| 441 | on the compiled path, the `ZZ32` `^` at a negative exponent breaks the integer power's contract: `2^(-1)` answers `0` where it throws `IntegerOverflow`, and `0^(-1)` throws a raw `java.lang.RuntimeException` that no Fortress `catch` sees, where it throws `DivisionByZero` | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (runtime) | `Specification/basic-lib/basic-integers.tex`, "Integers" | `ProjectFortress/compiler_tests/XXXIntegerPowerContractCompiled.test` | climb batch 6 rung T's skeptic | siblings: 523, 524, 525. `^` is bound as `jIntExp` (`ProjectFortress/LibraryBuiltin/CompilerBuiltin.fss:744`, at d4d454f9d) to `simpleIntArith.intExp`, which casts `Math.pow` to `int` (`ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/nativeHelpers/simpleIntArith.java:400-406`): a fraction truncates to 0, and `0^(-1)` is infinite and takes the overflow branch. The bases 1 and -1 give their power. Walk follows the contract (`IntegerPowerContractWalk`). The unbound `intToIntPower` (`:380-398`) answers 0 at `2^(-1)` and `0^(-1)` and raises `DivisionByZero` at every negative base (`perf-probes/integer-power/REPORT.md` §7). Workaround: a float base, `2.0^(-1)`. full text: history, row 441 |
| 457 | under `walk`, a total comparison `LEXICO Unordered` answers `Unordered`, where the specification gives the left argument: `LessThan LEXICO Unordered` and `GreaterThan LEXICO Unordered` are `Unordered` | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | library bug (library) | `Specification-1.0-frozen/advanced-lib/comparison.tex:163-177`, the trait `Comparison`'s `LEXICO` table, in `Specification-1.0-frozen/advanced-lib/advanced-lib.tex`, "Fortress APIs and Documentation for Library Writers" | `ProjectFortress/tests/XXXLexicoUnorderedRungH.fss` | climb batch 7 rung H | Only `Comparison`'s `opr LEXICO(self, other:Comparison) = Unordered` applies (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:138`, at d4d454f9d): `TotalComparison` declares `LEXICO` at a `TotalComparison` and at thunks only (`:177-179`). The lazy form follows the table: `LessThan LEXICO: Unordered` is `LessThan` (`compile-ladder/climb-batch-13/RECORD.md:174`), and a `()->Comparison` thunk reaches `:179` and `:214` (`ComparisonLazyLexico`). The prelude's body is the specification's (`ProjectFortress/LibraryBuiltin/CompilerBuiltin.fss:1494-1495`). Workaround: `LEXICO:`. full text: history, row 457 |
| 463 | on the compiled path, `if x <- e then … else … end` is refused, 'Variable __cond is not defined', and `while v <- e do … end` likewise for `__whileCond`, where walk runs both | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (prelude) | `Specification-1.0-frozen/basic/expressions/if.tex:29-34, 49`, "If Expressions"; `Specification/basic/expressions/while.tex`, "While Loops" | `ProjectFortress/compiler_tests/XXXIfGeneratorClause.test` | climb batch 7 rung H's skeptic | The `while` form's test is `XXXWhileGeneratorClause`. The pre-typecheck desugaring rewrites a generator-binding `if` into a call of `__cond` (`ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/compiler/desugarer/PreTypeCheckDesugaringVisitor.java:245-280`, at d4d454f9d) and the `while` into a call of `__whileCond` (`:306-317`). The interpreter's library declares both over `Condition[\E\]` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:1269-1273`); the compiler library declares neither, and with `__cond` declared in the program that refusal goes. Walk: `Just(3)` as the condition gives 13; the `while` prints `6`. Fix: declare both in `Library/CompilerLibrary.fss`. full text: history, row 463 |
| 465 | under walk, a program can call an overload that only the component declares, where the api declares others of the same name: on an `Array2`, `m[:, j]` and `m[i, :]` run, though the api's `Array2` declares neither subscript | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (walk) | `Specification/basic/components/source-code.tex`, "Import Statements" | none | climb batch 7 rung A | The component's `Array2` declares `opr[_:TrivialOpenRange, i1:ZZ32]` and `opr[i0:ZZ32, _:TrivialOpenRange]` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:2707`, `:2711`, at d4d454f9d); the api's `Array2` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fsi:1695-1732`) does not, and `VectorMatrixCastsWalk` runs both (`ProjectFortress/tests/VectorMatrixCastsWalk.fss:158-165`). A name only the component declares is refused: `__thrower`, 'Function __thrower is not defined' (probe `explorations/compile-ladder/rung-tabulate/probes/ComponentOnly.fss`, in the parent of `9269f7bd2`). Where walk takes the component's overloads over the api's is not located. Declaring them in the api makes the calls legal. full text: history, row 465 |
| 470 | the compiled checker does not check a static argument written at a top-level function call against its parameter's bound: it accepts `n[\String\]("d")` for `n[\T extends Number\](s: String): T` and `k[\(ZZ32, ZZ32)\]((1, 2))` for `k[\T extends Object\](x: T)`; walk accepts them too | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (checker) | `Specification-1.0-frozen/appendices/calculi/basic/static.tex:190-201`, "Static Semantics" (rule T-Invk, the premise at `:195`) | none | climb batch 7 rung B | A dotted call checks it (`staticInstantiationForApp`, `ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/scala_src/useful/STypesUtil.scala:803-832`, at d4d454f9d; F-70). By reading, a top-level call takes `staticInstantiation` (`scala_src/typechecker/impls/Functionals.scala:1191`), whose `staticArgsMatchStaticParams` matches kinds only (`STypesUtil.scala:782-801`), so a `where` constraint on the argument goes unchecked too (F-250). The checker checks the bound where it infers the argument (`k((1, 2))`) and in a type (`Box[\(ZZ32, ZZ32)\]`). No gated test can hold it (F-70). Probe `explorations/compile-ladder/rung-result-bounds/probes/compcheck/BoundCheck.fss`, gone. full text: history, row 470 |
| 495 | on the compiled path, overloaded generic dotted methods die at dispatch: `object K` with `grab[\X\](t: Tag[\X\])` and `grab[\Y\](t: Sub[\Y\])` dies on `K.grab(b2)` with `ClassCastException` (`Marker⟦Y⟧`), as does the swapped pair, and a pair at `ZZ32` dies on the run time's spelling of `ZZ32` | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (codegen) | `basic/overloading.tex`, "Principles of Overloading"; "Overloading Resolution" | `ProjectFortress/compiler_tests/XXXDispatchMethodArmRungG.test` | climb batch 6.5 rung G | siblings: 496. A method's dispatcher template is made on `OverloadSet`'s generic-function-class path, the trait's static parameters ahead of the method's (`ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/compiler/OverloadSet.java:2393-2414`, at d4d454f9d; `reviews/mie-probes/scope-call-site-dispatch.md` § 3, 'The remainder'). The call-site dispatch at the template's parameters that runs a top-level generic function's arms (`DispatchSwappedArmRungG`) does not reach it. An object's inherited generic arm beside a plain method dies in `readAndExpandGenericThing`, `IndexOutOfBoundsException` (`perf-probes/generic-distribute/REPORT.md` §7). full text: history, row 495 |
| 510 | under `walk`, a typed binding does not fix a static argument that only a call's result mentions: `a: BoxK[\ZZ64\] = wrapK(3)` is refused, 'RHS expression type BoxK[\Int\] is not assignable to LHS type BoxK[\ZZ64\]', and a method's `a: BoxT[\ZZ64\] = Fac.mk()` takes `BoxT[\Any\]` | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (walk) | `Specification/basic/expressions/var-ref.tex`, "Identifier References"; `Specification/basic/inference.tex`, "The Static Arguments of a Call" | `ProjectFortress/tests/XXXInferExpectedTypeRungK.fss` | climb batch N rung K | siblings: 705. `i: ZZ64 = idK(3)` answers `3 : ZZ64`, since the binding converts a result that is the type parameter itself, and no coercion converts a `BoxK[\Int\]` (F-55). The method form is `XXXExpectedTypeFixRungT`, which also asserts an assignment and a field declaration. A clause form takes the binding's declared type (F-228); a generic call does not. The compiled checker fixes `T = ZZ64` by the expected type. Repair: pass the declared type into the call's inference (`LHSEvaluator`, `BuildEnvironments`); whether before the switch-over (P-12) is the curator's. Workaround: `wrapK[\ZZ64\](3)`. full text: history, row 510 |
| 511 | under `walk`, a generic call over mixed widths through a varargs parameter `T...` or a tuple parameter `(T, T)` is not promoted: `varS[\T\](xs: T...)` with a `ZZ32` and a `ZZ64`, and `tupS[\T\](p: (T, T))` with `(z, w)`, run at the join, unconverted | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (walk) | the varargs shape: `Specification/basic-lib/basic-integers.tex`, "Integers"; the tuple shape silent | `ProjectFortress/tests/XXXInferVarargsRungK.fss` | climb batch N rung K's skeptic | siblings: 395. The first pass unifies a varargs argument, and a tuple type holding `T`, unpromoted (`ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/interpreter/evaluator/EvaluatorBase.java:606-610`, `:634-637`, at d4d454f9d). A lone `T` over tuples of mixed widths stays at the join too (`firstR(a: T, b: T)` with `((z, z), (w, w))`): `Coercions.coercionTargets` finds no coercion of a tuple (`values/Coercions.java:199-219`). So the pair order refuses mixed numbers at a position, `(1,2) < (1,2.5)` (`TupleOrderMixedRefused`). Compiled, the varargs program is refused, 'No such method T._[_]', and the tuple runs at the join. Workaround: `varR[\ZZ64\](z, w)`. full text: history, row 511 |
| 514 | the compiled path has no strided range: `seq(0:10:3)` is refused by the checker, "(ZZ32, ZZ32)->Range is not applicable to an argument of type (Range, IntLiteral)", while walk runs it (4 elements) | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | library gap vs spec (prelude) | `Specification/basic/expressions/ranges.tex`, "Ranges" | `ProjectFortress/compiler_tests/XXXStridedRangeRungT.fss` | climb batch N rung T | siblings: 661, 71, 74, 314. The compiler library declares `opr :(lo:ZZ32, hi:ZZ32)` and `opr #(lo:ZZ32, sz:ZZ32)` only (`Library/CompilerLibrary.fsi:173-174`, at d4d454f9d), so `0:10:3` parses as `(0:10):3`; with a `nat` stride `0:10:n` the same. The one library declares the strided `:` at `Range[\ZZ32\]`, which is no generator, so the checker refuses `seq(a:b:c)` against it too (row 661). The test is a `compile` `.test` pinned by `compile_err_contains`. Workaround: a `#` or `:` range and a stride computed in the body. full text: history, row 514 |
| 523 | the compiled `ZZ32` `^` overflows unchecked: `2^31` throws a raw `RuntimeException` that no Fortress `catch` sees, and `(-3)^21` and `(-2)^33` saturate to `-2147483648`, where each throws `IntegerOverflow` | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (runtime) | `Specification/basic-lib/basic-integers.tex`, "Integers"; `Specification/basic/operators/opr-overview.tex`, "Multiplication, Division, Modulo, and Remainder Operators" | `ProjectFortress/compiler_tests/XXXPowZZ32CompiledRungE.test` | climb batch 6.5b rung E's skeptic | siblings: 441, 524, 525. `^` is bound as `jIntExp` (`ProjectFortress/LibraryBuiltin/CompilerBuiltin.fss:744`, at d4d454f9d) to `simpleIntArith.intExp` (`ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/nativeHelpers/simpleIntArith.java:400-406`), which throws a plain `RuntimeException` above `Integer.MAX_VALUE` and casts the rest of a `double` to `int`, saturating below. Walk raises `IntegerOverflow` (`PowChooseLcmRungE`). Fix: bind a checked power in the compiler prelude. The unbound `intToIntPower` (`:380-398`) checks overflow, but breaks row 441's contract at a negative exponent (`perf-probes/integer-power/REPORT.md` §7). full text: history, row 523 |
